Date: Thu, 18 Mar 93 14:04:06 EST
From: david@WAL6000B.UDC.UPENN.EDU (R. David Murray)
Subject: THEOLOGY: (was The Internet Witch Trial)

Tom Morrow writes:
> Extropian theology might qualify as such a replacement.  Perhaps Tim should
> excuse Eric for buying into already existing memetic vaccines.  But as for
> myself, I'm going to custom craft one that will both feed my memetic hunger
> and boost my Extropian goals.

A while back there was a brief discussion on this list of 'religious
technology', and how some of it could be useful, but you had to strip it
of its religion-derived extra baggage.  Someone also commented that this
was harder than it looked, not so much because the claptrap was such a
large percentage of the 'operation', but because some of the stuff that
looks at first glance like claptrap actually serves some sort of
purpose.

What I think Eric's brand of neo-paganism is about is trying to extract
that religious technology into a usable form, without bringing along
the excess baggage.  As Tom pointed out, there are dangers there . . . 

Unless I remember/guess incorrectly, I believe it is also the case that
much of what Eric does is, indeed, /crafting/ custom tailored tools and
memes.

That said, I think it would be beneficial to do even more work on
finding out just what is essential to the technology and what is
baggage.  Eric probably agrees with that (but I can't speak for him
<grin>).

In other words, I don't think Tom's goals and Eric's goals are that far
apart, it's just that they are approaching the problem from opposite ends
(Tom: start from zero and add things rationally until you get a
satisfying meme set; Eric: start from a satisfying meme set and
rationally strip things away until you have a minimum satisfying set).

-- 
