Date: Thu, 18 Mar 93 12:28:14 PST
From: Robin Hanson <hanson@ptolemy.arc.nasa.gov>
Subject: META: Is an armed society a polite society?

Tom Morrow writes:
>Now of saying that Robin's
>Polite Society is marred by the inability to effectively impose costs on
>lurkers, I am going to suggest that a revision might allow it to do so
>after all.  What sort of revision?  Dumping tons 'o email into the lurkers'
>addresses if they are shot.  
>
>Why should lurkers care about getting overloaded in email?  Because, if
>done properly, it will interfere with their ability to lurk.  This would
>require that 1) we know the lurkers' real addresses, and that 2) we
>disguise bogus email so that it is indistinguishable from the real stuff,
>forcing the errant lurker to waste lots of time on decoys before finding
>the meaty email.

Well, like your temporary unsubscribing from the list idea, this is a
good idea if it could be implemented on a net-wide scale (for a
net-wide society) and not cause too much pain for list administrators.

But I worry that this idea is too much like many other good ones that
get posted here, which should work if only someone would go figure out
the details and then go implement it. 

Nick Szabo notes:
>Challenges must be signed with real addresses, which can be
>verified by an exchange between challengers or a judge.

This helps, but there's still lots more to do to make this workable.

Rather than let the best be the enemy of the good, I'd rather just
start with something we can do right now.  If that turns out to have
fatal problems, then fine, we'll have to do more work or scrap the
whole thing.  I claim my current proposal(s) are ready to be
implemented, requiring no extra coding or assistance from list
administration.

Derek Zahn suggests:
>One more thing -- I only join if a deanimated poster is allowed
>a brief "death scene" -- tearful goodbyes, lots of punctuation
>marks, a little ASCII blood, you know...

This was allowed for in the first version of the rules I proposed.

Dean Tribble comments:

>I'm here to talk about such extropian things, not defend them; I want
>this to be my haven, not the stalking grounds for bozos from another
>meme-set.  I have no interest in setting up a system in which people
>could zap me just because they don't like my reaction to their
>favorite religion.  

If people mainly just shoot folks because they don't like them, rather
than because they were impolite, then the polite society will have
failed, and I will quit.  (Anyone want to make bets on whether this
will happen?)  

But if politeness remains the main reason, then I suspect "bozos from
anothe meme-set" who show up here will much more the target of shots
than most folk.  This would then work against them, not you.

But maybe there are also better rules that could help.  How about
after getting 12 hits one is expelled from the Society?  A shooting
rampage would not go on forever then.

Or how about allowing messages to have a comment in the subject line
warning of impoliteness, like "(impolite)" similar to the common
"(long)" warning?  The idea would be that one couldn't be shot for
impolite posts which one warned readers about.  Or maybe whole lists
could declare themselves rudeness zones.

I'm trying to be open to change here, but I see folks anxious to get
started.  Send me private mail if you think I should stop negotiating
and just declare a final set of rules.  Without them, folks can't
really join.

Robin Hanson


