Date: Thu, 18 Mar 93 17:57:58 EST
From: sulko-m@acsu.buffalo.edu (Mark A. Sulkowski)
Subject: THEOLOGY: Religious meme receptor sites (R-sites)

T.O. Morrow writes:
>Extropians will, in any case, want to find the memetic defense that most
>fully supports their world view.

	At least until the point where we can modify the brain at will.
It may be possible to 'fix' what evolution has done to us in giving us
our religious meme receptor sites.  Perhaps if we rewire our neurons
just so...then we will end up with no particular desire or need for
religion whatsoever.  In a world of nearly guaranteed immortality, death
will not be nearly so worrisome, so the religious meme site may have
outlived its original purpose anyway.

	I have a pet theory that the reason morality is so closely related
to religion is that the powerful censoring effect of the R-site allows
someone to filter out "bad" thoughts and focus on "good" thoughts.  This
may actually have a survival advantage.  Consider an average atheist
compared to someone of a religion that promoted a "positive attitude".
It may be that while the atheist was a little more rational, the religious
person may have such an intensely reinforced positive attitude that it
may provide an advantage over the relatively negative atheist in most
ordinary life circumstances.

	Of course, the atheist may have the last laugh by getting a
cryonic suspension while the religious person happily goes toward her death.

	I'd love to see an analysis of the effects of various belief
systems on people's attitudes and behavior.  For example, comparing
Catholics and Protestants on success in business and attitudes towards
capitalism (perhaps even a measure of the levels of altruistic behavior).


>I sometimes wonder if Extropianism would not serve as a better defense
>against religious irrationality if it made more explicit the theological
>ramifications of transhumanism.

	I'll bet that is true.  I believe that extropianism needs to give
a sense of _Awe_.  I remember when I first started reading EXTROPY that
I immediately started to get excited about the ideas, in part because they
were so much like my own.  One thing that really boosted my excitement
to great heights was, of all things, the EXTROPY cover with the very
subtle 'star-burst'.  It had such symbolic power for me that I had a
experience of a rush of Awe and enthusiasm that seemed much like what I
have heard of the 'born-again' experience of Christians.


>But as for myself, I'm going to custom craft one that will both feed my
>memetic hunger and boost my Extropian goals.

	That sounds good, but I wish we knew more about what we are
dealing with.  We really need to advance the field of memetic research.


