Date: Thu, 18 Mar 93 15:52:36 -0800
From: tcmay@netcom.com (Timothy C. May)
Subject: Thalidomide Babies and the FDA

shunwton@reading.ac.uk writes (quoting someone else's comment):

>>            And most libertarians would like to see the approval
>>  process abolished altogether, replaced with private, votultary
>>  seals of approval.
>
>Try telling that to a thalidomide baby. Sounds like an excuse for
>drugs corporations to sell snake oil.

I would gladly tell a thalidomide-affected person (now about 30 years old)
that while what happened to them was tragic, the effects of the thalidomide
reaction on the drug approval process have been more tragic:

- tens of thousands of lives lost as valuable drugs have been held back by
years of "cover your ass" red tape at the FDA (the American drug regulation
agency).

- pain and suffering as new painkilling drugs (including marijuana, for
cancer nausea) are held back by a thalidomide-traumatized FDA

- essentially no system for allowing experimental treatments, even by the
fully informed.

- central approval is also susceptible to bribes and corruption, as seen in
the former USSR. And private credentially, a la Underwriter's Laboratories,
does just as effective a job of keeping dangerous equipment off the market
as the FDA could ever hope to do.

In conlusion, I would have no hesitation in making these points to a
thalidomide victim.

-Tim May
