Date: Thu, 18 Mar 93 19:37:36 GMT
From: jrk@information-systems.east-anglia.ac.uk (Richard Kennaway)
Subject: ECON/LAW: Copyrights

David Lubkin writes:
>Intellectual property is just an end case, where the material content
>is zero.
>
>Why should it be protected?  For all the usual reasons that any property
>is protected.  My basic right to own and control the fruits of my labor.

I think we part company here.  I do not believe in basic rights of any
sort.  Rights are social conventions, observed either because those
involved choose to observe them or because they are imposed by force.  I
also do not believe in an objective notion of property -- it too is defined
by conventions which people observe for the same sort of reasons.  There
may be particular rights and properties whose general observance we on this
list might all find desirable, but to simply state that when I create
information, I have created a piece of property which automatically belongs
to me is to beg all the interesting questions.

>And the very practical reason that if you do not allow people to own what 
>they produce, they will produce less than if you do.

In the case of intellectual property, there is however, the compensating
advantage of freer replication and distribution.  See also the area of
software patents, and the argument that allowing copyrighting or patenting
of user interfaces would be detrimental.

>And the right should no more expire than your ownership of land should
>expire.
>
>Richard argued that perhaps the right should vest exclusively with the
>creator, and should not be transferrable.  What is the social utility in
>making property rights transferrable?  It increases the incentive for
>people to create new wealth.  A property is worth more if its property
>rights are transferrable.  Many people are motivated by the desire to
>create wealth that they can transfer to their offspring, spouses, or
>charitable organizations.

That sounds fine, but there may be arguments the other way as well.  I
forget the legal details, but under certain regimes, an artist's right to
be identified as the author of his work cannot be waived.  The law deems it
to be inalienable.  I believe the reason for designing the relevant law in
that way was to redress (by statist means, but that's by the way) a power
imbalance between people such as screenwriters and film producers.  The law
prevents writers being pressured into selling for a pittance the right to
be credited, by deeming it not to be a sellable entity.

Whether the law achieves this effect in practice I do not know.  The point
is simply that there are counterarguments.

As for rights outliving the creator, the works of Aristotle are still in
print, in numerous editions and translations in all languages, despite
no-one owning the hypothetical abstract entity which they constitute. 
No-one has to pay any sort of licence fee for the information in
Aristotle's writings (as distinct from a particular edition of them). 
Similarly for any other author who has been dead a century.

If perpetual intellectual property rights were instituted, and Aristotle's
work in the abstract were deemed to be an intellectual object capable of
being owned, who would get to own it?  Will the next person to bring out a
scholarly edition of Aristotle be deemed to now own the abstract Aristotle,
by virtue of having "mixed his labour" with it.  Would he be able to demand
a fee from anyone else wishing to do research on Aristotle, even if they
don't use his edition?  How would anyone else benefit from this setup?  I
find the whole picture bizarre.  Where will the invention of such abstract
ownable objects end?  If I calculate and publish the first billion digits
of pi, can I claim ownership of pi itself by virtue of my work on this
hitherto unowned object?  If I prove a mathematical theorem, shall I be
able to claim ownership of it?  (The last question is not entirely
hypothetical, e.g. the RSA patent.)

>I don't buy the argument that you are not diminished by someone else
>making a copy of your work, so therefore it should be okay.  Let's 
>suppose I own a weekend cabin with a lake.  I am not diminished by your
>swimming in my lake during the week.  Nonetheless, I may forbid you 
>from using my property in that way.

Again, this is begging the question.  Land rights do not work that way
everywhere.  An English farmer is required to allow access to public rights
of way.  I have heard that owners of tracts of forest in Germany are
required to allow free access to the public.  By what notion of property do
you deem yourself to have a right to forbid me from harmlessly using your
property, and how do you justify it?

